00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Over on the blog this last week we had a post about developing or training youth leaders

37
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
And I heard from Mike Rossender about that post

38
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
And he said: in your driving example-

39
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
and part of the post is an example of how I taught my steps on to drive behind the wheel

40
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
of an old standard transmission Honda Civic.

41
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
But Mike went on to say you provided some critical training. specifically,

42
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
You shared some new vocabulary that made it easier for him to understand the cause

43
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
and effect of what was going on.

44
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
While I agree training can be easily overdone,

45
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
no training at all can leave novices with no mental handles,

46
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
no easy way to get a grip on the problem.

47
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
With a little bit of vocabulary,

48
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
they can think more efficiently about their experience.

49
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
The training you provided was minimal, targeted

50
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
and delivered exactly when it was needed.

51
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
That training should not be discounted, because I strongly suspect

52
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
that tiny bits of training made the experiential discovery

53
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
even more powerful, engaging and fun.

54
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Well, Mike, I think we probably agree more than we disagree.

55
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Now I'm a really hard sell on event based training, simply because

56
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
I have very little evidence that it's a very effective tool for developing leaders.

57
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
I think most of the event based training- and I'm talking about

58
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
hey, let's sit down and we're going to train you how to be a leader, kind of things-

59
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
I think, if we're brutally honest with ourselves,

60
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
it's driven more by the desire of adults to explain things than anything else.

61
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
I think we're more comfortable when we can explain what's going on,

62
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,000
and you know, we want our youth leaders to know everything that we know

63
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
about leadership all at once while they're sitting and listening.

64
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
And we want to introduce a vocabulary to them

65
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
And I don't think that's all that helpful, frankly.

66
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
If we begin with discovering the concepts behind the vocabulary,

67
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
then they'll pick up the vocabulary as they go along,

68
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
but the definitions will really stick

69
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
because they'll understand experientially what they mean.

70
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Now, explaining things isn't objectively bad, of course,

71
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
but I just think it's the least effective method for helping people understand

72
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
or develop skills in things like leadership.

73
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Some explaining may be helpful from time to time-

74
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
and I think I pointed that out in the example in that post-

75
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
But experiential discovery is a much more powerful way

76
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
to begin to develop and attain these skills.

77
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
So if we take all of the things, all of the concepts and ideas

78
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
that are in our event-based kind of formal training

79
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
and we make that part of the experiential process,

80
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
in giving our scouts limited and timely explanations

81
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
as a part of an experiential process,

82
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:22,000
then all of the content of the formal training is going to be built in,

83
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
but they're going to get it exactly when they need it

84
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
as they're actually doing the work.

85
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Everybody's mileage is going to vary on that.

86
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
I'm talking about the style that I prefer

87
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
and that's why I get to have a blog in a podcast.

88
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:40,000
But I really appreciate it, Mike, I appreciate your thoughts. I do.

